If you're shopping around for logo design, you'll quickly find that the price varies significantly — and so does what you actually get. Here's what a professional package should include.

## The logo itself — in multiple versions

A professional logo design doesn't deliver a single file. It delivers multiple versions of the logo to cover every use case:

- **Full colour version** — for use on white or light backgrounds
- **Reversed version** — white or light logo for use on dark backgrounds
- **Monochrome version** — black and white, for use in single-colour print contexts

Each version should be delivered in appropriate file formats.

## All necessary file formats

A complete logo package includes:

- **SVG** — vector format, scales to any size without quality loss
- **PNG** — with transparent background, for web and digital use
- **PDF** — vector format for professional print
- **JPEG** — for platforms that require it

If any of these are missing, you'll run into problems when you try to use the logo in different contexts. See our guide to [logo file formats](/blog/what-file-formats-do-you-need-for-your-logo/) for a full explanation.

## Brand identity elements

A professional package should include more than just the logo mark. It should also include:

**Colour palette** — the exact colours used in the logo, specified in hex (for web), RGB (for digital), and CMYK (for print). Without these specifications, colour consistency across different applications is almost impossible.

**Typography** — the typefaces selected for use alongside the logo, including where each should be used.

**Brand guidelines** — a brief document explaining how to use all of the above correctly, including spacing requirements, what not to do with the logo, and correct colour applications.

## Ownership

You should own the files outright, with no ongoing licensing fees or restrictions. This should be explicitly confirmed before you start.

